{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SZhBjBrkecJ2swhKA27wXG","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel01.3-uc001-du\n     2→title: \"Report recursive directory disk usage via du\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory to report disk usage. The\n     6→  differential testing harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary\n     7→  (gdu from coreutils) on the same directory on the same filesystem with the same arguments\n     8→  and environment, and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. Block counts\n     9→  are deterministic when both binaries stat the same files on the same filesystem. All tests\n    10→  use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform block-size default variance.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd009-du.yaml) and shared component PRDs\n    16→  (pkg/sys, pkg/format from rel00.0) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential\n    17→  test for cmd/du.\n    18→\n    19→flow:\n    20→  - id: F1\n    21→    step: |\n    22→      The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory with known file sizes and\n    23→      directory structure. Example: ./bin/du -k testdata/du-fixture/\n    24→  - id: F2\n    25→    step: |\n    26→      cmd/du traverses the directory tree using os.ReadDir (or filepath.Walk), calling\n    27→      pkg/sys.Lstat on each entry to obtain st_blocks for block counts and st_dev/st_ino\n    28→      for hard-link deduplication. It accumulates block counts per directory.\n    29→  - id: F3\n    30→    step: |\n    31→      cmd/du writes one line per directory (or per file with -a) in the format\n    32→      \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\". With -h, sizes are formatted via pkg/format.HumanSize.\n    33→  - id: F4\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/du and gdu with\n    36→      the same arguments, environment, and working directory.\n    37→  - id: F5\n    38→    step: |\n    39→      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n    40→      produce identical size values and path listings because they stat the same files on\n    41→      the same filesystem.\n    42→  - id: F6\n    43→    step: |\n    44→      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    45→      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n    46→\n    47→touchpoints:\n    48→  - T1: \"cmd/du — recursive traversal, block accumulation, hard-link dedup, flag modes (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n    49→  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat/Lstat for st_blocks, st_dev, st_ino, InstallSIGPIPEHandler\"\n    50→  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize for -h output\"\n    51→  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n    52→\n    53→success_criteria:\n    54→  - id: S1\n    55→    criterion: cmd/du compiles and ./bin/du exists after mage build.\n    56→  - id: S2\n    57→    criterion: |\n    58→      Default recursive mode produces one line per subdirectory and the root directory,\n    59→      matching gdu -k output on the same fixture.\n    60→  - id: S3\n    61→    criterion: |\n    62→      -s summary mode produces one line per argument with the total size, matching gdu -sk\n    63→      output.\n    64→  - id: S4\n    65→    criterion: |\n    66→      -a all-files mode produces one line per file and directory, matching gdu -ak output.\n    67→  - id: S5\n    68→    criterion: |\n    69→      -h human-readable mode produces size strings (K/M/G) that match gdu -h output on the\n    70→      same fixture.\n    71→  - id: S6\n    72→    criterion: |\n    73→      -c grand total mode produces a \"total\" line after all arguments, matching gdu -ck output.\n    74→  - id: S7\n    75→    criterion: |\n    76→      Hard-link deduplication works: a fixture with two directory entries pointing to the same\n    77→      inode produces a total that counts the file's blocks only once, matching gdu behavior.\n    78→  - id: S8\n    79→    criterion: |\n    80→      --apparent-size mode uses st_size instead of st_blocks, matching gdu --apparent-size\n    81→      output.\n    82→  - id: S9\n    83→    criterion: |\n    84→      The differential test passes for all flag combinations: default -k, -sk, -ak, -h,\n    85→      -ck, -d 1, --apparent-size, and missing path.\n    86→\n    87→out_of_scope:\n    88→  - Cross-filesystem behavior (files on different mount points) is not tested in the\n    89→    differential harness; both binaries see the same single filesystem.\n    90→  - SIGPIPE handling is specified in prd009-du R5.1 but is not directly testable in the\n    91→    differential harness.\n    92→  - Sparse file creation depends on the filesystem; --apparent-size tests use a regular file\n    93→    with known size rather than a true sparse file.\n    94→\n    95→test_suite: test-rel01.3\n    96→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2111581c-5ab1-41cd-b38c-38140798a1e8","uuid":"c98052e6-21f7-4c4d-a632-535d2620a824","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/237/docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.3-uc001-du.yaml","content":"id: rel01.3-uc001-du\ntitle: \"Report recursive directory disk usage via du\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory to report disk usage. The\n  differential testing harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary\n  (gdu from coreutils) on the same directory on the same filesystem with the same arguments\n  and environment, and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. Block counts\n  are deterministic when both binaries stat the same files on the same filesystem. All tests\n  use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform block-size default variance.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD (prd009-du.yaml) and shared component PRDs\n  (pkg/sys, pkg/format from rel00.0) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential\n  test for cmd/du.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory with known file sizes and\n      directory structure. Example: ./bin/du -k testdata/du-fixture/\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      cmd/du traverses the directory tree using os.ReadDir (or filepath.Walk), calling\n      pkg/sys.Lstat on each entry to obtain st_blocks for block counts and st_dev/st_ino\n      for hard-link deduplication. It accumulates block counts per directory.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      cmd/du writes one line per directory (or per file with -a) in the format\n      \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\". With -h, sizes are formatted via pkg/format.HumanSize.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/du and gdu with\n      the same arguments, environment, and working directory.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n      produce identical size values and path listings because they stat the same files on\n      the same filesystem.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/du — recursive traversal, block accumulation, hard-link dedup, flag modes (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat/Lstat for st_blocks, st_dev, st_ino, InstallSIGPIPEHandler\"\n  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize for -h output\"\n  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: cmd/du compiles and ./bin/du exists after mage build.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      Default recursive mode produces one line per subdirectory and the root directory,\n      matching gdu -k output on the same fixture.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      -s summary mode produces one line per argument with the total size, matching gdu -sk\n      output.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      -a all-files mode produces one line per file and directory, matching gdu -ak output.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      -h human-readable mode produces size strings (K/M/G) that match gdu -h output on the\n      same fixture.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      -c grand total mode produces a \"total\" line after all arguments, matching gdu -ck output.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      Hard-link deduplication works: a fixture with two directory entries pointing to the same\n      inode produces a total that counts the file's blocks only once, matching gdu behavior.\n  - id: S8\n    criterion: |\n      --apparent-size mode uses st_size instead of st_blocks, matching gdu --apparent-size\n      output.\n  - id: S9\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test passes for all flag combinations: default -k, -sk, -ak, -h,\n      -ck, -d 1, --apparent-size, and missing path.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - Cross-filesystem behavior (files on different mount points) is not tested in the\n    differential harness; both binaries see the same single filesystem.\n  - SIGPIPE handling is specified in prd009-du R5.1 but is not directly testable in the\n    differential harness.\n  - Sparse file creation depends on the filesystem; --apparent-size tests use a regular file\n    with known size rather than a true sparse file.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel01.3\n","numLines":96,"startLine":1,"totalLines":96}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKUVoq2vnZHg5J1ECVT1fn","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n     2→// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n     3→\n     4→//go:build darwin || linux\n     5→\n     6→// Package sys wraps Darwin and Linux syscalls and signal handling. It provides\n     7→// a stable interface that cmd/ packages use to avoid platform-specific code in\n     8→// utility implementations.\n     9→//\n    10→// Implements prd002-sys (R2).\n    11→package sys\n    12→\n    13→import (\n    14→\t\"fmt\"\n    15→\t\"time\"\n    16→\n    17→\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n    18→)\n    19→\n    20→// FileMetadata provides platform-independent access to file metadata fields\n    21→// that diverge between Darwin and Linux (modification time from\n    22→// st_mtimespec/st_mtim, disk block count from st_blocks).\n    23→// R2.2, R2.3: abstracts Darwin/Linux stat struct divergence.\n    24→type FileMetadata struct {\n    25→\tmodTime time.Time\n    26→\tblocks  int64\n    27→}\n    28→\n    29→// ModTime returns the file's modification time extracted from the underlying\n    30→// platform-specific stat struct (st_mtimespec on Darwin, st_mtim on Linux).\n    31→// R2.2: modification time accessor.\n    32→func (m *FileMetadata) ModTime() time.Time {\n    33→\treturn m.modTime\n    34→}\n    35→\n    36→// Blocks returns the number of 512-byte blocks allocated for the file,\n    37→// extracted from st_blocks in the underlying stat struct.\n    38→// R2.2: disk block count accessor.\n    39→func (m *FileMetadata) Blocks() int64 {\n    40→\treturn m.blocks\n    41→}\n    42→\n    43→// Stat returns file metadata for the named file, following symbolic links.\n    44→// Equivalent to os.Stat but populates platform-specific fields via\n    45→// golang.org/x/sys/unix.\n    46→// R2.1: stat with symlink resolution.\n    47→func Stat(path string) (*FileMetadata, error) {\n    48→\tvar stat unix.Stat_t\n    49→\tif err := unix.Stat(path, &stat); err != nil {\n    50→\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"stat %s: %w\", path, err)\n    51→\t}\n    52→\treturn newFileMetadata(&stat), nil\n    53→}\n    54→\n    55→// Lstat returns file metadata for the named file without following symbolic\n    56→// links. Equivalent to os.Lstat but populates platform-specific fields via\n    57→// golang.org/x/sys/unix.\n    58→// R2.1: lstat without symlink resolution.\n    59→func Lstat(path string) (*FileMetadata, error) {\n    60→\tvar stat unix.Stat_t\n    61→\tif err := unix.Lstat(path, &stat); err != nil {\n    62→\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"lstat %s: %w\", path, err)\n    63→\t}\n    64→\treturn newFileMetadata(&stat), nil\n    65→}\n    66→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2111581c-5ab1-41cd-b38c-38140798a1e8","uuid":"9cdfcdae-c069-4067-900e-b7ea1298620a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/237/pkg/sys/fileinfo.go","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n//go:build darwin || linux\n\n// Package sys wraps Darwin and Linux syscalls and signal handling. It provides\n// a stable interface that cmd/ packages use to avoid platform-specific code in\n// utility implementations.\n//\n// Implements prd002-sys (R2).\npackage sys\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n)\n\n// FileMetadata provides platform-independent access to file metadata fields\n// that diverge between Darwin and Linux (modification time from\n// st_mtimespec/st_mtim, disk block count from st_blocks).\n// R2.2, R2.3: abstracts Darwin/Linux stat struct divergence.\ntype FileMetadata struct {\n\tmodTime time.Time\n\tblocks  int64\n}\n\n// ModTime returns the file's modification time extracted from the underlying\n// platform-specific stat struct (st_mtimespec on Darwin, st_mtim on Linux).\n// R2.2: modification time accessor.\nfunc (m *FileMetadata) ModTime() time.Time {\n\treturn m.modTime\n}\n\n// Blocks returns the number of 512-byte blocks allocated for the file,\n// extracted from st_blocks in the underlying stat struct.\n// R2.2: disk block count accessor.\nfunc (m *FileMetadata) Blocks() int64 {\n\treturn m.blocks\n}\n\n// Stat returns file metadata for the named file, following symbolic links.\n// Equivalent to os.Stat but populates platform-specific fields via\n// golang.org/x/sys/unix.\n// R2.1: stat with symlink resolution.\nfunc Stat(path string) (*FileMetadata, error) {\n\tvar stat unix.Stat_t\n\tif err := unix.Stat(path, &stat); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"stat %s: %w\", path, err)\n\t}\n\treturn newFileMetadata(&stat), nil\n}\n\n// Lstat returns file metadata for the named file without following symbolic\n// links. Equivalent to os.Lstat but populates platform-specific fields via\n// golang.org/x/sys/unix.\n// R2.1: lstat without symlink resolution.\nfunc Lstat(path string) (*FileMetadata, error) {\n\tvar stat unix.Stat_t\n\tif err := unix.Lstat(path, &stat); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"lstat %s: %w\", path, err)\n\t}\n\treturn newFileMetadata(&stat), nil\n}\n","numLines":66,"startLine":1,"totalLines":66}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015nSf7GoygvGSEHrYWrRWmB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to check the platform-specific files and see if there's anything about Dev/Ino in FileMetadata. The PRD mentions sys.FileInfo with Dev, Ino, etc. but the actual fileinfo.go only has modTime and blocks. Let me check the platform-specific files.\n\nAlso, I need to check if there's an InstallSIGPIPEHandler in pkg/sys. The PRD says R5.1 mentions it, but the task description says to use the same goroutine pattern as cmd/cat (which just does signal.Notify directly in main). Let me look more carefully.\n\n\nLooking at the requirements, I need to use os.ReadDir for traversal and call pkg/sys.Lstat to get the 512-byte block count through FileMetadata.Blocks(), then convert to 1024-byte units with the formula (rawBlocks + 1) / 2. The issue is that pkg/sys.FileMetadata only exposes modTime and blocks—there's no Dev or Ino fields, and the task doesn't actually require hard-link deduplication anyway.\n\nLet me check the platform-specific implementations to see the full API surface.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5678,"cache_read_input_tokens":50266,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5678},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2111581c-5ab1-41cd-b38c-38140798a1e8","uuid":"3b9114e6-12df-444b-8281-7ef2bcf47e10"}
